README.OSK - BRU/OS-9 1.2 on OS-9/68000========================================BRU/OS-9 was written by Michael R. Sweet in 1991 for OS-9/6809, with the1.1 and 1.2 additions (-s restart, /dd/sys/bru.dev) by Gene Heskett.  Thisis that source built for OS-9/68000 with the Microware C compiler.  Theoriginal files are unchanged in the archive this came from; every changemade here is listed below and is marked in each file's revision history,as paragraph 2 of COPYING requires.Build-----    cc bru_main.c bru_misc.c bru_file.c bru_backup.c bru_restore.c       bru_list.c -qm=16k -DOSK -n=bru -f=bru(one line).  -DOSK selects every change below; without it the source isthe 6809 original.What changed, and why---------------------1. bru.h: <lowio.h> is a 6809 header that OSK does not have.  Under OSK,   <modes.h> is included instead and READ, WRITE, DIR (S_IREAD, S_IWRITE,   S_IFDIR), FRONT and END (lseek origins 0 and 2), TRUE and FALSE are   defined from it.2. bru.h: fd_t and header_t are byte-exact under OSK.  The 68000   compiler puts a short or a long on an even offset, so the original   declarations came out as 18 bytes and 260 bytes (measured), where the   program needs 16 (what _gs_gfd() and _ss_pfd() move) and 256 (one   archive record).  Under OSK every multi-byte field is a char array --   fd_owner[2], fd_size[4], h_start[4] -- so the structures have the   6809 layout exactly.  An archive written here has the same format as   one written by BRU/OS-9 on a 6809.3. bru.h and bru_misc.c: because of 2, the few places that read fd_size   or read and write h_start go through FD_SIZE(), H_START() and   SET_START().  Without OSK these are the plain field; with it they call   bru_get4() and bru_put4(), two small functions added at the end of   bru_misc.c that read and write a big-endian long as four bytes.   (They are functions, not macros, because the macro form made one line   of bru_backup.c long enough to stop Microware's cpp.)  The call sites   changed are in bru_backup.c, bru_file.c, bru_restore.c and   bru_list.c.4. bru_backup.c: header.h_fd.fd_link corrected to fd_links, the name the   field has in bru.h.  The 6809 compiler accepted the misspelling   because <direct.h> declares an fd_link at the same offset; the 68000   compiler does not.5. bru.h: patmatch() and ltoa() are 6809 library calls that the OSK   library does not have.  patmatch(pattern, name, TRUE) becomes the   kernel's own wildcard compare, _cmpnam() (F$CmpNam): '*' and '?',   case ignored.  It was measured on whole pathnames before it was used:   "src/s?b/*" and "*a" match "src/sub/a", "src/x*" and "src" do not.   ltoa() becomes sprintf(s, "%ld", n).6. bru_file.c, bru_show(): the owner is printed as group.user, from the   two owner bytes of the file descriptor, where the 6809 printed one   number through itoa() (which the OSK library also lacks).7. bru_file.c, bru_create(): a restored directory is given its owner   differently.  The original switched to the owner with asetuid(),   made the directory, and switched back.  OSK has no asetuid(), and   setuid() away from the super-user cannot come back (measured).  So   the directory is made as the super-user, then opened and its owner   bytes set through its file descriptor (_gs_gfd() and _ss_pfd()).   Only the owner is changed; as before, a directory's dates are those   of the restore.  If the directory already exists it is left alone,   as before.Nothing else was changed: not the options, the messages, the archiveformat, or the behaviour described in bru1_1.doc and bru1.2_adds.Using it--------BRU runs for the super-user only (anyone else gets exit status 214,E_FNA, and no message).  The archive is named with -f, which must comebefore the paths; without it BRU uses the name in /dd/sys/bru.dev, or/d1/BRUfile.  Without -q it asks for a disk ("Insert next disk and pressENTER") before its first read or write, so give -q when the archive is afile.  Paths are stored as typed, and restored relative to the currentdata directory:    chd /dd/work    bru -c -q -f /dd/save.bru mydir        back up mydir    bru -t -q -vvv -f /dd/save.bru         list it, long form    chd /dd/elsewhere    bru -x -q -v -f /dd/save.bru           restore all of it here    bru -x -q -f /dd/save.bru "mydir/sub/*"   restore part of itRestoring by pattern creates only what matches, so the directoriesabove a matching file must already exist.  A second -c to the samearchive appends to it.What was tested, on os9exec---------------------------A tree of two directories, a text file in each and a 5322-byte binary,with owners 3.7 and 5.9 and four different attribute sets, was backedup, listed, and restored into another directory; every restored filecompares equal to its original, and dir -e shows the same owner,attributes and file date on each.  Also: restoring over an existingcopy, restoring by pattern, appending a second backup to an archive,-n with a date and with a file, and refusal for a user who is not thesuper-user.  This has not been tried on real OS-9/68000 hardware, andmulti-disk archives (a disk filling during -c) have not been tried.Things to know--------------- Dates are in OS-9's own form, the year counted from 1900, so a year  after 1999 is 100 or more.  The -vvv listing prints each date field as  two digits and shows such a year as "<6" for 126 (2026); this is in  the original code.  For the same reason -n wants the year as OS-9  stores it: -n 126/09/01, not 26/09/01 (which matches everything).- The archive file is created with owner write permission only, as on  the 6809.  The super-user can read it regardless; use attr to add read  permission if anyone else is to read it.- The usage message does not mention -f; bru1_1.doc does.